#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:2
msgid "Documentation in scientific and academic publishing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:4
msgid ""
"On this page, we explore some of the many tools and practices that "
"*software* documentation and *academic* writing share. If you are working"
" within the field of science or academia, this page can be used as an "
"introduction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:23
msgid ""
"Documentation and technical writing are broad fields. Their tools and "
"practices have grown relevant to most scientific activities. This "
"includes building publications, books, educational resources, interactive"
" data science, resources for data journalism and full-scale websites for "
"research projects and courses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:27
msgid ""
"Here's a brief overview of some :doc:`features </reference/features>` "
"that people in science and academic writing love about Read the Docs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "🪄 Easy to use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:32
msgid ""
"Documentation code doesn't have to be written by a programmer. In fact, "
"documentation coding languages are designed and developed so you don't "
"have to be a programmer, and there are many writing aids that makes it "
"easy to abstract from code and focus on content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:34
msgid "Getting started is also made easy:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:36
msgid ""
"All new to this? Take the official :external+jupyterbook:doc:`Jupyter "
"Book Tutorial <start/your-first-book>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:37
msgid "Curious for practical code? See :doc:`/examples`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:38
msgid "Familiar with Sphinx? See :doc:`/guides/jupyter`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid ""
"🔋 Batteries included: Graphs, computations, formulas, maps, diagrams and "
"more"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:42
msgid ""
"Take full advantage of getting all the richness of "
":external+jupyter:doc:`Jupyter Notebook <index>` combined with Sphinx and"
" the giant ecosystem of extensions for both of these."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:44
msgid "Here are some examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:46
msgid ""
"Use symbols familiar from math and physics, build advanced proofs. See "
"also: `sphinx-proof <https://sphinx-"
"proof.readthedocs.io/en/latest/syntax.html>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:47
msgid ""
"Present results with plots, graphs, images and let users interact "
"directly with your datasets and algorithms. See also: `Matplotlib "
"<https://matplotlib.org/stable/tutorials/introductory/usage.html>`__, "
"`Interactive Data Visualizations "
"<https://jupyterbook.org/en/stable/interactive/interactive.html>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:48
msgid ""
"Graphs, tables etc. are computed when the latest version of your project "
"is built and published as a stand-alone website. All code examples on "
"your website are validated each time you build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "📚 Bibliographies and external links"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:52
msgid ""
"Maintain bibliography databases directly as code and have external links "
"automatically verified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:54
msgid ""
"Using extensions for Sphinx such as the popular `sphinxcontrib-bibtex "
"<https://sphinxcontrib-bibtex.readthedocs.io/>`__ extension, you can "
"maintain your bibliography with Sphinx directly or refer to entries "
"``.bib`` files, as well as generating entire Bibliography sections from "
"those files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "📜 Modern themes and classic PDF outputs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:61
msgid ""
"Use the latest state-of-the-art themes for web and have PDFs and e-book "
"formats automatically generated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:63
msgid ""
"New themes are improving every day, and when you write documentation "
"based on Jupyter Book and Sphinx, you will separate your contents and "
"semantics from your presentation logic. This way, you can keep up with "
"the latest theme updates or try new themes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:65
msgid ""
"Another example of the benefits from separating content and presentation "
"logic: Your documentation also transforms into printable books and "
"eBooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "📐 Widgets, widgets and more widgets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:69
msgid ""
"Design your science project's layout and components with widgets from a "
"rich eco-system of open-source extensions built for many purposes. "
"Special widgets help users display and interact with graphs, maps and "
"more. :external+jupyterbook:doc:`Several <content/components>` "
"`extensions <https://sphinx-gallery.github.io/>`__ are built and invented"
" by the science community."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "⚙️ Automatic builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:73
msgid ""
"Build and publish your project for every change made through Git (GitHub,"
" GitLab, Bitbucket etc). Preview changes via pull requests. Receive "
"notifications when something is wrong. How does this work? Have a look at"
" this video:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "💬 Collaboration and community"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:89
msgid ""
"Science and academia have a big kinship with software developers: We ❤️ "
"community. Our solutions and projects become better when we foster "
"inclusivity and active participation. Read the Docs features easy access "
"for readers to suggest changes via your git platform (GitHub, GitLab, "
"Bitbucket etc.). But not just any unqualified feedback. Instead, the "
"*code* and all the tools are available for your community to forge "
"qualified contributions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:91
msgid "Your readers can become your co-authors!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:93
msgid ""
"Discuss changes via pull request and track all changes in your project's "
"version history."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:95
msgid ""
"Using git does not mean that anyone can go and change your code and your "
"published project. The full ownership and permission handling remains in "
"your hands. Project and organization owners on your git platform govern "
"what is released and who has access to approve and build changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "🔎 Full search and analytics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:99
msgid ""
"Read the Docs comes with a number of features bundled in that you would "
"have to configure if you were hosting documentation elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:101
msgid "Super-fast text search"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:102
msgid ""
"Your documentation is automatically indexed and gets its own search "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:104
msgid "Traffic statistics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:105
msgid ""
"Have full access to your traffic data and have quick access to see which "
"of your pages are most popular."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:107
msgid "Search analytics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:108
msgid ""
"What are people searching for and do they get hits? From each search "
"query in your documentation, we collect a neat little statistic that can "
"help to improve the discoverability and relevance of your documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:110
msgid "SEO - Don't reinvent search engine optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:111
msgid ""
"Use built-in SEO best-practices from Sphinx, its themes and Read the Docs"
" hosting. This can give you a good ranking on search engines as a direct "
"outcome of simply writing and publishing your documentation project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst
msgid "🌱 Grow your own solutions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:115
msgid ""
"The eco-system is open source and makes it accessible for anyone with "
"Python skills to build their own extensions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:117
msgid ""
"We want science communities to use Read the Docs and to be part of the "
"documentation community 💞"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:120
msgid "Getting started: Jupyter Book"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/science.rst:122
msgid ""
":external+jupyterbook:doc:`Jupyter Book <intro>` on Read the Docs brings "
"you the rich experience of computed `Jupyter <https://jupyter.org/>`__ "
"documents built together with a modern documentation tool. The results "
"are beautiful and automatically deployed websites, built with Sphinx and "
":doc:`Executable Book <executablebook:index>` + all the extensions "
"available in this ecosystem."
